when I was living in Tokyo I would  sometimes just try to escape the super  busy cityscape and go to one of the  ancient gardens that are scattered  throughout the city and really they're  one of the only places in Tokyo where  you can actually get a taste of what  Tokyo used to be hundreds of years ago  and where you can escape that super busy  city life because even though Tokyo has  been flattened over the years by  earthquakes and Wars and the Japanese  still have this idea that buildings  around the temporary and so this whole  city is just modern skyscrapers  everywhere the gardens are just about  the only old remaining parts of what  japan used to look like hundreds of  years ago so there's something really  cool to visit I definitely recommend  checking out at least one of them when  you're in Tokyo and since I visited  pretty much all of them I have this  obscene amount of footage then I still  wanted to do something with so yeah  let's go ahead and check out some of my  favorite gardens in Tokyo the first  thing that I wanted to start with brings  us to Shinjuku Station because I really  want to see Shinjuku Gyoen this is  possibly one of the most famous gardens  it's been depicted in so many movies  I think Makoto Shinkai is a big fan of  this garden and the surroundings you can  actually see the skyline of Shinjuku  from the garden but I think the the best  part about this this whole park is the  traditional old Japanese garden that's  in here it is a very sort of standard  garden aside from the fact that it's  extremely busy it's definitely one of  the most popular ones in the city it  centered around this large pond with  these traditional pine trees and Okada  comi these trimmed the bushes around it  what's special is that it has this  really cool Taiwanese pavilion and it's  really an amazing thing to look out at  the garden from traditionally Japanese  gardens don't do this Chinese thing  where you look at the garden from inside  a building but in this building you  actually have some of the best views of  the whole garden so it's a bit of a  Chinese touch to it  I was also lucky enough to go here  during the perfect Momiji or a Japanese  maple autumn season so the trees were  all beautifully red and just one of my  favorite things about Japanese garden  and switch this garden does really well  is that they use all kinds of different  trees which bloom and lose their leaves  at different times of the year which  means that you always get this broad  palette of different colors in autumn  what's also interesting is that it  features the pavilion from the garden of  words which is a really famous Makoto  Shinkai  anime film so it's cool to visit that  for a second and kind of look out at the  lake from that pavilion the same way  that the characters from the movie did  it although I think in the end my  favorite place of it is still the time  when these people win anyway with that  said that's just about a very quick  overview of Shinjuku Gyoen which is  located right in the middle of this  super bustling city area which is really  cool to visit otherwise as well  definitely one of my favorite parts if  not my favorite part of the city but  yeah I think it's time we move on to the  next place which is Kyushu buddy'  cunanan  which is not as well-known but it's  known as one of the most beautiful  gardens for sure it's quite small and  it's in this very skyscraper filled  Harbor area over here but it's it's one  of the best protected gardens I think it  burned down completely in the past but  it's been completely restored to its  former glory it's basically a very  prototypical example of a strolling  garden with a pond in the middle so the  whole garden is more or less just a  single path that goes along or around  this enormous pond in the middle with  all kinds of scenes alongside it like  these pillars there is a beautiful  bridge going over the middle of the lake  there are some stone lanterns it's very  sort of standard fare but I think this  garden is probably the best garden at  presenting all these signature typical  elements of Japanese landscapes in a  super small amount of space because this  garden really is absolutely tiny  and within Tokyo it's actually my  personal favorite for me the only thing  that kind of brings it down even though  it's sometimes really cool like seeing  that train in the background it's just  it's too much into this whole area of  the city where it doesn't really feel  like it belongs all the skyscrapers kind  of pull you out of the natural  environment of the Garnon which i think  is a little bit of a shame but it's it's  also a very unique kind of way to have  this gone and the only thing that really  is a shame about it is that supposedly  you should be able to see the sea from  this garden but as you can tell you  really can't see just buildings so yeah  that's just a little bit of a shame but  I don't want to bring this gone and down  with that at all if anything it's just a  beautiful oasis right in the middle of  this super busy area near the harbor now  on a very short walk from this garden  through Schiano may you can arrive at  hama rikyu garden which is very similar  name and i think quite a few internet  pages actually kind of mess these two  things up but there are very different  gardens how many queue is much much  bigger it's right next to the co-domain  area with some of the biggest  skyscrapers in tokyo and it has this  enormous lake which you can not just  walk around but which has this really  beautiful tea house in the middle of the  lake which I think works out really well  actually with that super enormous  contrast between the skyscrapers in the  background and all of the water in the  foreground of the garden so I think in  this case the whole mix between the  garden and the skyscrapers works out  just a little bit better if just because  the environment is a little bit a little  bit less messy but also just because  this gun is so much larger it features a  lot of different little ponds many trees  some areas even where you just walk  through these little winding paths you  kind of go through bamboo forests and  you have on these trees around the past  which is really cool  some of the more sort of laid-back and  less busy parts of the garden but I  should say this is definitely one of the  most popular gardens in the city as well  I think it shows up on a lot of tourist  guide maps and you could definitely see  a lot of people here and as you can see  it's also a really really beautiful  garden to visit when it's the autumn  season and all the trees are losing  their leaves and especially as the Sun  sets you get some really cool views of  this garden and I think it's also here  where I saw some of the most interesting  trees like this tree which has like a  split down the middle which the Sun  comes through here and there's also this  really really cool Japanese maple tree  right like this one which also has an  insane sky going on and it's just the  colors are just so bright it's really  cool to see this also nearby is Naga  King capsule Tower so if you're an  architecture nerd you can check that out  as well it's a little bit dilapidated  but oh well and there's also Hayao  Miyazaki's clock over here in shioma  which is a really cool part of Studio  Ghibli right in the middle of an office  district another garden that wasn't so  high on my list but then I visited  because it was nearby Thunder Dolphin  the roller coaster as you can see here  is korakuen garden which is really close  to Tokyo Dome city and is also another  one of these real ancient Edo era  gardens in Tokyo which is a lot more  forested it's much more dense and  features less of these or Conoco many  bushes but more of these general forests  and this beautiful red bridge with  really cool leaves around here and it  much more than many of the other gardens  it feels less like a walk around a large  pond and more like a walk through a  really cool forest even though I think  still a lot of the same principles still  apply it's really a sort of miniature  creation of natural scenes which is  really the focus of a lot of Japanese  gardens to create our natural kind of  environments in a very miniature setting  it also features this really cool bridge  which is really cool to see now the last  garden which I want to feature here  which unfortunately I forgot to record  when I went to it earlier  the cookie and garden and the reason why  this clip is kind of interesting is  because this is in winter after there  was a lot of snowfall and yeah that was  a lot of snow so it was really cool to  see a Japanese garden in this snow since  it's the setting that you know usually  see them in but they work out really  well regardless a lot of these gardens  are made to look different but still  look good in every single setting and  you'll notice that for instance these  stone lanterns look really cool and  there's a small layer of snow on them  and these traditional tea houses are  just so cozy it's really cool to see in  any case these are my recommendations  for awesome Tokyo Gardens the visits  I'll definitely get into more nerdy talk  about the background of Japanese gardens  again in my Japanese garden series in  planet coaster but I hope that it was  enjoyable to still see these clips of  real live Japanese gardens which were  definitely the inspiration and main  motivation to try and make one myself in  planet coaster as well I hope you  enjoyed and thanks for watching
 

